# C++ Development Guidelines

## Language Standards
- **C++20**: Use modern C++ features freely (no compatibility shims)
- **Build System**: Bazel with Clang18
- **Common Dependencies**: absl, catch2, nlohmann_json

## Naming Conventions
- **Variables/Functions**: `snake_case`
- **Classes/Structs**: `PascalCase`
- **Constants/Macros**: `ALL_CAPS`
- **Files**: `snake_case` (e.g., `checkpoint_store.cc`)

## Code Style
- **Headers**: Use `#pragma once` for header guards
- **Includes**: Group in order: own headers, standard library, third-party, project headers
- **Namespaces**: Use nested namespace declarations (C++17 style)
- **Auto**: Use `auto` for complex types, explicit types for primitives
- **Const**: Prefer `const` and `constexpr` wherever possible

## Functions & Methods
- Start function names with a verb
- Use structs for multiple parameters/returns
- Prefer default parameters over overloading
- Use trailing return types for template functions

## Classes & Objects
- Follow Rule of Five or Rule of Zero
- Make member variables private with getters/setters as needed
- Use `= default` and `= delete` explicitly
- Prefer composition over inheritance
- Declare interfaces as abstract classes or concepts
- Avoid using forward declarations for classes

## Error Handling
- Use `absl::Status` or `absl::StatusOr<T>` for error handling
- Never use exceptions in core library code
- Check status values immediately after calls
- Provide meaningful error messages

## Memory Management
- Always use RAII principles
- Avoid manual memory allocation

## Concurrency
- Use absl thread annotations (`ABSL_GUARDED_BY`, etc.)
- Prefer task-based parallelism over thread-based
- Use `std::atomic` for lock-free programming
- Document thread safety guarantees

## Testing
- Use Catch2 framework
- Follow Arrange-Act-Assert pattern
- One test per behavior
- Use `SECTION` for test variations

## Documentation
- Use Doxygen style for public APIs
- Document preconditions and postconditions
- Include usage examples in header comments
